verdict:  
I was completely wrong and barking up the wrong tree for 20 minutes, I didn't spend a good few minutes expanding my candidates and tried so hard to make nxf8 work but missed a quiet move and stopped calculating after the queen trade. After f3 after the queen trade on d8, white's king is in a lot of danger. I assumed I had time to take the g pawn but didn't calculate it properly. 

post reveal analysis of Qd7 (haven't looked at the variation yet)

Qd7, Rc7, Qe8+, Nf8, Rd8 (threatening mate in 2), Rf5, Nxf8, Rxf8, Qe6+, Kh8, Qxh3 +1 } 1. Qd7! {The correct move, a move I didn't even consider because I didn't put enough **effort** into expanding my candidates, I only considered 2 candidates. I started choosing candidates with CCT but stopped at captures and didn't look for threats! so in the future I should consider all checks captures AND threats when I go into a long think in a messy and double edged position like this where everything hangs on one move. }  (1. Nxf8 {This is the line I saw and spent 15 minutes on, but was just wrong at the end because I stopped calculating even as forcing moves still existed. }  hxg2  (1... Kxf8  2. Qd8+) 2. Qd5+ Kxf8 3. Qd8+  (3. Qd6+  Kf7 4. Qd5+ Kg7) Qxd8 4. Rxd8+ Ke7 {I stopped calculating here! but if i had calculated just 1 or 2 plies further I'd realise my rook is under attack and needs to spend 1 tempo defending it after ke7, then f3! comes and my king is locked in and getting mated. I just assumed I'd win a tempo with the check and then take on g2. Lesson: it's worth calculating a line out as far as you can if the sequence is very forcing. In this case it was. }) Rf7 2. Qe8+  (2. Qc8+  Nf8 3. Rd8 Rhf5 4. Nxf8 Rxf8 5. Qxf5 Rxd8 6. gxh3 {this is the variation the book gives, which is also winning but in my opinion unecessarily complicated compared to my line, my variation where the queens are also traded seems practically much easier to convert}) Nf8 3. Rd8 Rhf5 4. Nxf8 Rxf8 5. Qe6+ Kh8 6. Rxf8+ Rxf8 7. Qxh3 {upon seeing the correct first move after getting it incorrect, I correctly calculated the sequence to the end! So the lesson is, don't skimp on expanding the candidates during a long think} Qxh3 8. gxh3 Kg7 9. Re4 Rf5 10. Kg2 *

